==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|satiatus|lang=la}}, past participle of {{term|satiare||to fill full, satiate}}, from {{term|sat}}, {{term|satis||sufficient}}, {{term|satur||full}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-satiate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|satiat|ing}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[fill]] to [[satisfaction]]; to [[satisfy]].
#: ''Nothing seemed to '''satiate''' her desire for knowledge.''
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[satisfy]] to [[excess]]. To fill to [[satiety]].

====Usage notes====
Used interchangeably with, and more common than, {{term|sate|lang=en}}.<ref>“[http://blog.oup.com/2011/11/nov-gleanings/ Monthly Gleanings: November 2011]: ''Sate'' versus ''satiated''.”, ''[http://blog.oup.com/ OUPblog]''</ref>

